From 43897dbbfc45bc8fa5ee5a44d4e3c9d408c831e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 13:47:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08064/11362] [outer-loop] security audit follow-up: make the
 gVisor sandbox reachable

`sandbox_docker.run_in_docker` was exported from `carnot.autoresearch` and
invoked by NOTHING. `orchestrator.py` called the in-process `run_in_sandbox`
unconditionally at all three execution sites (278, 425, 615) and contained zero
references to CARNOT_USE_SANDBOX. So for the autoresearch pipeline -- the one
component whose job is executing LLM-generated code -- the container boundary
was unreachable, and setting the documented env var did nothing.

This is the same shape as the bug that opened this audit: a control that reads
as available and never fires. It matters more than an ordinary wiring defect
because fix 2/5 established that the in-process sandbox is NOT a security
boundary -- it has a known, still-open reflective escape via
object.__subclasses__(). The container was the only real containment and it
could not be switched on.

Adds execute_hypothesis() as the entry point and routes the orchestrator's three
call sites through it. Env-var semantics are COPIED from the existing convention
in carnot/verify/python_types.py rather than inventing a third spelling:

  CARNOT_USE_SANDBOX=1      prefer the container; warn loudly and fall back to
                            in-process if Docker/image is missing, so a dev box
                            without Docker still runs
  CARNOT_REQUIRE_SANDBOX=1  demand the container; FAIL CLOSED if unavailable and
                            do not execute the hypothesis at all
  neither                   in-process, exactly as before -- the default is
                            unchanged, so this adds no Docker dependency

Availability is checked BEFORE launching rather than inferred from a failed
result. run_in_docker returns failure both when Docker is missing and when the
hypothesis legitimately fails; treating any failure as "infrastructure
unavailable" would re-run the code in-process -- double execution, and it would
drop isolation precisely when the code is misbehaving. Once the container is
launched its verdict is final.

Also corrects the sandbox docstring, which named CARNOT_USE_SANDBOX without
saying what had to call it. It now states that calling run_in_sandbox() directly
always runs in-process and ignores both variables. That wording was mine, from
fix 2/5, and it was misleading in exactly the way this commit fixes.

9 new tests. test_orchestrator_calls_the_dispatcher_not_the_backend is a
source-level assertion because this is a wiring bug: run_in_docker was exported
and called by nothing for as long as it existed, and no behavioural test would
have caught that. Mutation-proven -- reverting the orchestrator to
run_in_sandbox makes it fail. 38 pass across the sandbox suites.

Adds REQ-SEC-003.
